<div class="body" id="fhbody-186551391">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186551391" class="p">
			
		 	
				Binance now says it's replenished 90% of assets stolen last week in a $6.3 billion security breach, its  customers still "rushed to move funds off the platform" when it reopened Sunday, <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/02/binance-outflow-hack-bitcoin.html">CNBC reports</a>, and Binance "said it recorded $3.64 billion in net outflows after reopening withdrawals on Sunday."

<i>
The exchange processed $4.65 billion in withdrawals and $1.01 billion in deposits between 6 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. UTC on Nov. 1, Binance <a href="https://x.com/binance/status/2116926305254133892">said</a> in a post on X.   The resulting net outflows represented 2.1% of Binance's $171 billion in total assets, based on its latest available proof-of-reserves data as of Oct. 28.  Binance said withdrawal volumes had declined and were stabilizing following an initial spike, while deposit volumes remained substantial. <br> <br>
The exchange began restoring withdrawals in phases at 6 a.m. UTC on Sunday, with services resuming across all 15 networks and assets included in the initial phase by 12:36 p.m. UTC. It said testing was continuing for the remaining networks and assets.<br> <br>

Binance founder and former CEO Changpeng Zhao sought to reassure users following the breach, <a href="https://x.com/cz_binance/status/2116954044988354798">writing</a> on X that "98% choose to stay!" He said most of the net outflows occurred within the first couple of hours after withdrawals resumed.
</i><br>
		 	
