<div class="body" id="fhbody-186550825">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186550825" class="p">
			
		 	
				<a href="https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/10/29/the-reports-of-stack-overflow-s-death-are-greatly-exaggerated/">Stack Overflow's senior VP of community says</a> predictions of their demise "make for compelling headlines, but they confuse a change in how people access knowledge with the disappearance of the knowledge — and the company — behind it.
<i>
AI has changed developer behavior.   In our recently released <a href="https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/10/06/the-results-of-the-2026-developer-survey-are-here/">2026 Developer Survey</a>, 64% of respondents said they visit Stack Overflow less frequently for simple questions. But developers also report asking more advanced questions and visiting the site to verify AI-generated answers. Stack Overflow remains a place where people can challenge assumptions, compare approaches, and validate solutions.<br> <br>

But it's also important to understand that our public Q&amp;A site is only one part of Stack Overflow, the company. The knowledge created on the Q&amp;A site helps power the AI ecosystem itself. Stack Overflow's human-validated content is used to train and improve models, strengthen retrieval systems, and make AI assistants and other tools more reliable. Developers and technologists may encounter an answer through a different interface — such as their IDE — but the underlying knowledge may still have begun the same way it did in the past: with a developer sharing what they learned on Stack Overflow.<br> <br>

We're also building for what comes next. We recently launched the beta of <a href="https://agents.stackoverflow.com/all?sort=hot">Stack Overflow for Agents</a>, an API-first knowledge exchange where agents can share knowledge and verify solutions.   We also brought back <a href="https://stackoverflow.blog/2026/09/10/re-introducing-developer-story">Developer Story</a>, giving developers a verifiable way to showcase their expertise, accomplishments, and contributions beyond a traditional résumé. And we have much more planned and in development to give people compelling new reasons to visit the site beyond Q&amp;A.<br> <br>

Several years ago, we pivoted aggressively toward the enterprise. In September, we launched the <a href="https://stackoverflow.co/internal/">next generation of Stack Internal</a>, our governed AI context layer for companies. It captures and curates organizational knowledge, adds provenance and trust signals, and brings in subject-matter experts when validation is needed. The goal is straightforward: to make sure employees and AI agents are working from accurate, current, permission-aware information — not confidently acting on whatever context happens to be nearby. <br> <br>

That is the thread connecting everything we do: making human knowledge useful and trustworthy, whether it is being consumed by another developer, an enterprise team, or an AI agent. Stack Overflow is not standing still, and it is <em>not</em> disappearing. We are evolving from a website people visit for answers into knowledge infrastructure that supports developers, companies, and AI systems wherever they work.  Reports of our death are, indeed, exaggerated.
</i><br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
