</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186549813">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186549813" class="p">
			
		 	
				SystemD creator Lennart Poettering left Red Hat for Microsoft <a href="https://www.theregister.com/software/2022/07/07/lennart-poettering-leaves-red-hat-for-microsoft/517193">in 2022</a>, but <a href="https://linux.slashdot.org/story/26/01/30/235231/author-of-systemd-quits-microsoft-to-prove-linux-can-be-trusted">10 months ago</a> left to co-found Amutable a company that aims to build cryptographically verified Linux systems that stay trusted over time. <br> <br>

<em>It's FOSS</em> calls it "a read-only, image-based system where every machine produces signed reports of its own state" — and signups are now open for a private preview:
<i>
Before announcing this Linux-based implementation, Amutable had released two open source tools. <a href="https://github.com/amutable/tine">tine</a>, a Buck2-based build system that composes OS images; available under MPL-2.0, and <a href="https://github.com/amutable/quarry">Quarry</a>, a TUF-based tool that distributes those images to machines; available under Apache-2.0.  What Amutable Foundations brings to the table is a service where Amutable runs the images, update repositories, control plane and vulnerability fixes for organizations and individuals who operate their own systems. <br> <br>

No license has been stated for those company-run parts, and <a href="https://amutable.com/blog/introducing-amutable-foundations">the preview announcement</a> does not say how much of Foundations beyond tine and Quarry will be open source.   Software and configuration both ship as signed images, and a machine boots only what is signed. Because there is <strong>no package manager and no SSH or interactive shell</strong>, a change means giving every machine in your fleet a new signed image.<br> <br>

Its images are reproducible, come with a software bill of materials (<a href="https://www.cisa.gov/topics/information-communications-technology-supply-chain-security/sbom">SBOM</a>) and are built from pinned inputs of whichever distribution you pick, with tine being able to import packages from Fedora or CentOS Stream.  Each machine sends a signed report that records what it booted, from the firmware and boot loader up to the kernel and OS, plus the configuration it runs...  Amutable's control plane verifies reports from <a href="https://amutable.com/blog/it-starts-upstream-systemd-report">systemd-report</a>, an experimental command in systemd 260 that the company helped build. It compares each one with the declared state and flags machines that deviate.  A rollout only continues while machines can show they are in a good state...<br> <br>

Amutable is <a href="https://amutable.com/preview">signup form</a>looking for teams that manage Linux fleets and need to show what state each machine is in. It expects to start bringing the first '<em>design partners</em>,' as it calls them, on board over the coming weeks.   </i><br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
