<div class="body" id="fhbody-186523434">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186523434" class="p">
			
		 	
				<a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/29/meta-staff-us-social-media-critic-jonathan-haidt-court-documents">The Guardian reports</a>:
<i>Meta staff discussed deploying a "whisper strategy" to undermine the influential US academic whose work has inspired a global <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/oct/29/id-social-media-ban-snapchat-tiktok-teenagers">social media crackdown</a>, according to court documents. Employees at the parent group of Facebook and Instagram sought to discredit Jonathan Haidt, the social psychologist and author of <a href="https://link.amazon/B01e7lnxN">The Anxious Generation</a>, a book that helped tip the debate about online safety towards more stringent measures such as the under-16 ban in Australia. <br> <br>

Minutes of internal meetings contained in a recent legal filing in the US showed that staff discussed how to "get the academic community to disavow Haidt". They <a href="https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.cand.401490/gov.uscourts.cand.401490.3604.6.pdf">appear in a document</a> titled "Well-being Meeting Sound-check Notes", which refers to discussions of how Meta could "turn other academics against Haidt" and to a "whisper strategy" aimed at achieving that goal. It also mentions "debunking" his work.  Minutes from a meeting on 8 March 2023 mention "Curtiss", an apparent reference to Meta's then vice-president of research, Curtiss Cobb. It states: "Curtiss's goal for this work: how do we get the academic community to disavow Haidt. Can't co-opt them, so how do we enrage them ... so the media finds him not credible."<br> <br>

It then adds: "Not thinking about this as a loud strategy. A below-the-line campaign...."<br> <br>

The minutes were produced before Haidt published his book in 2024 and discuss responding to a <a href="https://www.afterbabel.com/p/the-teen-mental-illness-epidemic">Substack post written by the academic</a> in which he reviews "a bunch of articles". </i> <br>

"It is not clear whether Meta acted on the ideas outlined in the minutes."<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
