<div class="body" id="fhbody-186538879">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186538879" class="p">
			
		 	
				Clocks "fall back" one hour tonight to end Daylight Saving Time — though many Americans want to end the twice-yearly clock change, <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/10/30/trump-fall-back-daylight-saving-time-debate">reports Axios</a>.<br> <br>

But the U.S. Congress "remains deadlocked about how to make that happen."

<i>The clocks are falling back despite a bipartisan House <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/26/07/15/0346241/house-votes-for-permanent-daylight-saving-time">vote in July to make daylight saving time permanent</a> and Trump's subsequent <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/25/10/29/0321225/senator-blocks-trump-backed-effort-to-make-daylight-saving-time-permanent">push for Senate action</a>... While most Americans want to end the twice-yearly clock changes, they don't necessarily favor Trump's solution.  <a href="https://news.gallup.com/poll/657584/half-daylight-saving-time-sunsetted.aspx">Gallup</a> found 48% of Americans support permanent standard time the entire year, compared with 24% who prefer permanent daylight saving time, as Trump wants... In 1974, the U.S. experimented with year-round daylight saving time but reversed course amid concerns about children going to school <a href="https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/RS22284.html">in the dark&gt;.  </a><br> <br>

Experts still debate whether permanent DST is good for people. Supporters argue that permanent daylight saving time would provide more afternoon and evening sunlight, encouraging outdoor activities.  But sleep experts warn that darker mornings and lighter evenings can disrupt sleep cycles, while permanent daylight saving time could force millions of children to travel to school in the dark.  The bottom line: The American Academy of Sleep Medicine says it prefers <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10758561/">permanent standard time</a> because it better aligns with natural sleep cycles.</i> <br>

"Hawaii and most of Arizona don't observe daylight saving time, so their clocks won't change," the article points out, adding that eliminating it for the rest of America "still faces opposition in the Senate and isn't expected to be considered by the end of the year.<br> <br>

      But "the upcoming midterms could change the balance of power in Congress, potentially shifting the debate."<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
