		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  EditorDavid
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186541157" datetime="on Saturday October 31, 2026 @11:34PM">on Saturday October 31, 2026 @11:34PM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">AI-everywhere</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186541157">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186541157" class="p">
			
		 	
				Should generative AI be used for videogame development?  Brandon Stirrup, a strategy manager at Xbox, claims Xbox is <em>already</em> using AI, <a href="https://www.engadget.com/2302431/brandon-stirrup-xbox-manager-says-ai-used-game-development/">reports Engadget</a>.  And he also says that those who say they aren't are merely "pretending."


<i> Stirrup deactivated his social media account shortly after (though his posts have been <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/pcmasterrace/comments/1x9u1aw/xbox_senior_business_strategy_manager_confirms/">archived elsewhere</a>), igniting conversations about what role generative AI actually has or should have in game development.  In some ways, it would be unsurprising to learn that Xbox is using AI to the extent that Stirrup implies. Xbox recently <a href="https://news.xbox.com/en-us/2026/10/13/introducing-xbox-labs/">announced</a> the formation of Xbox Labs at least in part as a response to a rising trend of AI-coded game ports; the announcement heavily implied that the new team would use AI as well but didn't say it outright. Xbox owner Microsoft is also a major player in AI development... Developers including Capcom, Square Enix, Sega, Nexon and Activision have all either used AI in their games or pledged to do so. Fortnite developer Epic Games has been particularly up-front about its AI use, and CEO Tim Sweeney has aggressively promoted it on social media... <br> <br>


On the other hand, there are large pockets of the game industry vocally opposed to generative AI. Notably, these tend to be smaller, independent studios without shareholders to impress by touting the cost-savings of AI. These studios include <a href="https://www.engadget.com/2018-06-11-tunic-first-look-xbox-zelda-e3-2018.html"> <em>Tunic</em></a> publisher Finji, <a href="https://www.engadget.com/gaming/nintendo/stardew-valley-players-on-the-nintendo-switch-2-get-a-free-upgrade-174849697.html"> <em>Stardew Valley</em></a> developer ConcernedApe and <a href="https://www.engadget.com/clickolding-from-the-i-am-your-beast-devs-looks-like-a-clicking-nightmare-180504296.html"> <em>Clickolding</em></a> developer Strange Scaffold, among many more. Due to the sheer number and variety of game studios, it's impossible to say just how much of the industry actually uses AI at this point, but claims that "everyone" is using it are clearly false.<br> <br>

There's a similar divide among players about whether generative AI should be embraced or avoided at all costs. While some simply accept AI as part of games going forward, others have pledged to avoid games that use the technology outright due to its environmental costs, labor issues or pure aesthetic preference. That's led to the popularity of tools like a <a href="https://github.com/seeeeew/aiwarningforsteam">browser extension</a> that makes Steam's AI disclosures more visible and grassroots efforts by developers to implement labels <a href="https://www.polygon-treehouse.com/no-gen-ai-seal">certifying their games as AI-free</a>...  Many players and developers alike justify the use of AI in generating code, even if they're opposed to it in other areas.</i> <br>

"Gaming is the only industry slow-walking the transition," Xbox's Stirrup <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/pcmasterrace/comments/1x9u1aw/xbox_senior_business_strategy_manager_confirms/">allegedly said</a>, "because users have negative sentiment. Everyone else accepts reality."<br> <br>

"Gaming is the only industry pretending to not use AI."<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
